Parameterized Base Constructor – Person and Student

📌 Description

This C++ program demonstrates Single Inheritance using two classes: "Person" and "Student".

The "Student" class inherits from the "Person" class and adds its own data member, "rollNumber".

---

📊 Class Structure

Person
   ↓
Student

---

📚 Classes Used

1. "Person" Class

The "Person" class is the base class.

class Person

It contains:

- "name" – Stores the person's name.
- Constructor – Initializes the name.
- The "name" variable is declared as "protected", allowing the derived "Student" class to access it directly.

---

2. "Student" Class

The "Student" class inherits from "Person" using public inheritance:

class Student : public Person

It contains:

- "rollNumber" – Stores the student's roll number.
- Constructor – Initializes the name and roll number.
- "display()" – Displays the student's details.

The "Student" constructor calls the "Person" constructor to initialize the inherited "name".

---

⚙️ Main Function

A "Student" object is created:

Student student("Kiran", 24);

Where:

- ""Kiran"" → Student's name
- "24" → Student's roll number

The "display()" function is then called:

student.display();

---

🖥️ Output

Name: Kiran
Roll Number: 24

---

🧠 OOP Concept Used

Single Inheritance

Person
   ↓
Student

Here:

- "Person" → Base class
- "Student" → Derived class
- Inheritance Type → Single Inheritance

The "Student" class reuses the "name" member from "Person" and adds its own "rollNumber" member.

